---
layout: changelog
title: Announcing Appwrite 2.4 for self-hosted deployments
date: 2026-10-08
cover: /images/changelog/2026-10-08.avif
tags: auth, messaging, console, security, infrastructure
---

Appwrite 2.4 is now available for self-hosting. This version includes important security updates. We strongly recommend upgrading your instance to apply these fixes.

This release includes:

- Custom profile photos. Users can upload and remove their own photo, and it is served ahead of the OAuth2, Gravatar and initials fallbacks
- Webflow as an OAuth2 provider
- Push messages straight to a user with the Appwrite push provider, without registering a device target
- Webhook delivery IDs that stay the same across retries, and retries that only resend to the endpoints that failed
- Outbound requests to user-supplied URLs refuse private addresses, with `_APP_ALLOWED_INTERNAL_ADDRESSES` to allow specific hosts
- User JWTs that only work in the project that issued them, and OAuth2 callbacks checked against the browser that started the flow
- Retention for ClickHouse's own logs, so idle installs stop growing by hundreds of MB a day
- Appwrite Console 1.2.55, with profile photos, the Webflow provider, CLI and MCP onboarding, and the breached password policy

The Appwrite Assistant is retired in this release. Before upgrading, check the [upgrade notes](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.4.0) if you run behind a public load balancer, use the GitHub integration, or reach private hosts for OAuth2, webhooks or messaging.

See the [migration guide](/docs/advanced/self-hosting/production/updates#upgrading-to-latest) to upgrade your instance.

For the complete list of updates and fixes, check out the [release notes](https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite/releases/tag/2.4.0) on GitHub.

